A few patterns worth seeing all at once:
The two DQC gates are the trust checkpoints. Input DQC stands between "data is in Igloo Cloud" and "the project is tagged as loaded for this period" — actuaries never see data that failed validation, because the run never reaches INPUT_STATUS = 'LOADED' if DQC blocks. Output DQC stands between "data is in Snowflake landing" and "files ship to MFP" — downstream consumers never receive numbers that didn't pass validation, even though the Snowflake landing tables get populated either way (so a data engineer can inspect them).
The two success badges (LOADED, COMPLETED) are the two contract states. Anyone querying the registry can answer "is the BENEFIT run for 2024-12 LOADED?" or "is it COMPLETED?" with a single column lookup. These are the meaningful states for downstream systems, dashboards, and ops — everything else in between is internal pipeline mechanics.
The failure handler treats two kinds of failures differently. Technical failures (Snowflake unavailable, Igloo API 5xx, SFTP timeout) page ops because retrying or fixing infra resolves them. DQC blocker failures page the data team because no amount of retrying makes bad source data correct — someone has to fix the source. Both end up as FAILED in IC_REG_RUN, but IC_RUN_STATUS_HISTORY.CHANGED_BY_TXT (input_pipeline / poller / dqc_gate) routes the alert to the right responder.
The gap between input close-out and the output poller is where Igloo Cloud — and the actuaries — own the data. That gap can last hours or days. The pipeline doesn't poll the actuaries; the next 30-min cycle just keeps looking, and the moment a run hits FINALIZED, the very next poll picks it up. No coordination needed between the two pipelines other than the shared registry.
